  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft and southerly winds at the surface \nhave allowed plenty of warm, moist air to move into the Red \nRiver Valley and points east. Temps have stayed in the upper 40s\nand even low to mid 50s even with plenty of cloud cover and \ndrizzle. Some spots are seeing visibility dip to around a mile, \nbut at this point probabilities for dense fog are less than 20 \npercent so will just include a mention in the grids for now. The\nwarm advection will give way to a cold front pushing in from \nthe northwest tonight and into tomorrow. The main upper low will\nbegin to dig into the Northern Plains Saturday and Saturday \nnight, then pull into the Upper Midwest on Sunday. Even more \ncold air coming down behind that system with northwesterly flow \naloft to start the upcoming work week. Another shortwave moving \nthrough Tuesday, then brief ridging mid-week gives way to near \nzonal flow with ensemble members all over the place with minor \nshortwaves. \n\n...Snow showers in the north tomorrow night...\n\nDrizzle and light rain will continue through this evening ahead\nof the cold front, and some rain showers will linger behind the\nboundary into tonight mainly in the north. At this point \ntemperatures look to remain warm enough to keep the \nprecipitation as rain. Better chances for snow will come as the \nmain upper low arrives Saturday night into Sunday, along with a \nreinforcing push of colder air. Probabilities of at least a \ntenth of an inch of snow are 60 percent or greater along and \nnorth of Highway 2. Chances for an inch or more are more spotty \nand long the Canadian border. With more showery nature to the \nprecipitation and warm ground temperatures don't think there \nwill be winter impacts everywhere. However, METRo plots have \nsurface temps dropping well below freezing Saturday night, and \nthose locations that see heavier snow rates could get a bit of \naccumulation to impact travel. Not near advisory criteria, but \ncould be enough impacts to surprise people with the mild fall so\nfar so will put out some messaging. \n\n...Colder temperatures Sunday and into next week...\n\nWhile the cold air coming down behind the upper low Sunday will\nbe a big change from current and recent temps, it will not be \ncompletely out of season for late November. Average highs are \nstarting to drop below 30 degrees as the days go on, and \nreadings are expected to be more in that range rather than 20 \ndegrees above. This fits with the mean ensemble pattern and the \nECMWF EFI's middle of the road temperature signal for next week.",
